---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro'
import Command from '../../components/Command.astro'
import { doc, repo } from '../../lib/content'
const sections: [string, string][] = [
  ['two-levels', 'Lanes and subagents'],
  ['enable', 'Enable Swarm'],
  ['team', 'Choose your team'],
  ['review', 'Inspect and review'],
  ['case-study', 'Remote Hosts case study'],
  ['boundaries', 'Limits and permissions'],
]
---

<Guide
  title="Multi-agent work & Swarm"
  description="One lane owns the mission. Subagents handle bounded pieces. You stay close to the decisions, the evidence, and the final diff."
  sections={sections}
  eyebrow="Workflow / from mission to reviewed change"
>
  <section id="two-levels">
    <h2>Two levels of parallel work</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Several lanes:</strong> separate tasks, each with its own parent agent and, by default in
      a Git repo, an isolated worktree. The home board helps you find what needs attention.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Several subagents inside a lane:</strong> the parent delegates focused work through the
      optional pi-subagents package, answers scope questions and integrates results. A child is not another
      top-level lane. Child isolation and workflow policy belong to pi-subagents, not a second Phosphor
      scheduler.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="enable">
    <h2>Authorize proactive delegation</h2>
    <p>
      Use a Phosphor build that includes the Swarm switch. Updating the app does not retrofit its
      command into an already-running Pi process.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        Install <code>pi-subagents</code> from Settings → Extensions if it is not already available. Start
        a new lane or reload/restart Pi in an existing one.
      </li>
      <li>
        Open the lane’s composer and turn on <strong>Swarm</strong>. The highlighted switch, amber
        outline and title badge reflect Pi’s acknowledgement, not just a local preference.
      </li>
      <li>
        Give the parent a goal, ownership boundaries, acceptance criteria and a verification plan.
        Swarm authorizes delegation when useful; it does not immediately launch a team.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <Command cmd="/phosphor-swarm on" label="Enable Swarm for this lane" />
    <p>
      This slash command is an alternative to the switch, not a shell command. If it is missing, the
      lane’s Pi process needs a reload/restart. Activation is unavailable when the pi-subagents tool
      surface is missing or excluded.
    </p>
    <p>
      A change during a run stays pending until a safe turn boundary. Swarm survives resume and
      compaction on that session branch. Forks, clones and Rewind start off under their new session
      identity.
    </p>
    <h3>Off is not Stop</h3>
    <p>
      Turning Swarm off removes its standing authorization from future requests. It does not cancel
      existing children, erase past messages, remove already-loaded tools or undo token spend.
      Explicitly requested delegation and project-authorized delegation still follow normal rules.
      Use the existing Stop controls when you want execution to stop.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="team">
    <h2>Choose models for the job</h2>
    <p>
      For example, use <strong>Astra</strong> to explore the architecture, <strong>Opus 5.5</strong> to
      orchestrate and integrate, and <strong>Sol 6.1</strong> for scoped implementation and separate reviewer
      runs. These are example assignments, not automatic routing, default presets or a claim that one
      model is always best.
    </p>
    <p>
      The parent model is selected in Phosphor. Child models and thinking levels are selected
      through pi-subagents’ run options or configured role policies. Do not assume a child inherits
      the parent’s live thinking level. Only ask for models available in your pi configuration.
    </p>
    <p>
      Claude Pro/Max can supply Opus through <code>pi-claude-cli</code> and the Claude Code CLI. Pi still
      owns the tools and agent loop; Phosphor is not a separate Claude model client. <a
        href="/guides/accounts/">Set up providers and subscriptions</a
      >.
    </p>
    <h3>A mission brief you can adapt</h3>
    <pre
      class="command"><code>Build the machine-enrollment feature.

Plan the boundaries first. Use Astra for architecture exploration,
Opus 5.5 for orchestration, and Sol 6.1 for implementation and
independent review if those models are configured. Ask before substituting.

Split independent work into enrollment API, Host lifecycle,
Desktop status, and docs. Give each worker owned files and
acceptance criteria. Require approval for shared-file changes.

Preserve work and decisions if a worker fails. Review each result
independently, integrate accepted changes, then validate the whole.
Do not deploy or merge without my approval.</code></pre>
    <p>
      This is a prompt example, not an installer or a built-in workflow. The parent chooses concrete
      tool calls under the available extension’s contract.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="review">
    <h2>Inspect work, not just activity</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The current-work summary separates parent activity from reported foreground and background
        work. Task links open the corresponding inspection, even when another pane was open.
      </li>
      <li>
        The Subagents pane keeps navigation separate from scrolling activity. Dock, resize or
        fullscreen it without making a new runner.
      </li>
      <li>
        Missing status is <strong>unavailable</strong>, not proof of completion. A long-running tool
        is not automatically stalled. Attention signals are addressed to the parent.
      </li>
      <li>
        A completed execution is not independent acceptance, and accepted pieces are not proof that
        the integrated change passes. Keep those checkpoints separate.
      </li>
      <li>
        Save a bounded inspection snapshot when you want to retain that evidence in Artifacts.
        Review the final diff and the project’s checks before approving a merge.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href={doc('chat.md#sub-agents')}>Inspection, steering, Stop and snapshot contract</a></p>
  </section>
  <section id="case-study">
    <h2>Real example: Phosphor Remote Hosts</h2>
    <p>
      The homepage uses selected records from Phosphor’s own Remote Hosts lane. Astra and Opus 5.5
      appear in the parent’s recorded model metadata at different stages; Sol 6.1 was assigned to
      workers and independent reviewers. This was explicit delegation before the Swarm switch
      existed, not a demonstration of that switch running.
    </p>
    <p>
      In one recorded phase, the parent coordinated four pieces: enrollment API, Host lifecycle,
      Desktop live status and documentation. A worker requested an exception to its owned files for
      shared shutdown behavior. The parent approved a minimal hook with unchanged defaults and
      tests. Another worker lost its connection; a replacement resumed retained work with the prior
      decisions in its brief.
    </p>
    <p>
      The public implementation trail includes <a href={`${repo}/pull/427`}>the control schema</a>, <a
        href={`${repo}/pull/428`}>Linux credential storage</a
      >, <a href={`${repo}/pull/429`}>Desktop machine approval</a>, <a href={`${repo}/pull/430`}
        >the combined site/API server</a
      >, and <a href={`${repo}/pull/435`}>Host identity</a>. These are evidence of individual
      changes, not a claim that every Remote Hosts capability is complete or deployed.
    </p>
    <h3>What the image represents</h3>
    <p>
      It is a <strong>curated replay in the actual renderer</strong>, not an untouched live
      screenshot. The excerpt is shortened, section headings are editorial, task labels are
      expanded, and identifiers, local paths, unrelated projects and deployment details are omitted.
      The four workstreams’ historical activity comes from the parent’s recorded report. No timing,
      token savings or speedup benchmark is claimed. No private transcript is distributed.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section id="boundaries">
    <h2>Power without hidden promises</h2>
    <p>
      Worktrees separate changes, not permissions. Agent tools run with your access unless you
      install a permission gate. More agents can consume more tokens, memory and provider quota.
      Swarm adds no hard spawn budget, cloud runner, automatic model assignment or automatic
      approval to deploy or merge.
    </p>
    <p>
      Remote-access development is separate from local delegation. Check the <a
        href={doc('remote-access.md')}>current remote-access contract</a
      > before treating a planned capability as available.
    </p>
    <p>
      The exact Swarm state, branch and timing rules live in <a
        href={doc('chat.md#swarm-authorization')}>Swarm authorization</a
      >. For installation, see <a href="/guides/extensions/#subagents"
        >the subagents package guide</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
</Guide>
